.TH VDSO 7 "2026-11-20" "SKYIOUS Kernel v0.3.0" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
vdso \- Virtual Dynamic Shared Object (SKYIOUS Kernel)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <vdso_libc.h>
.PP
.B int clock_gettime(clockid_t clk_id, struct timespec *tp);
.PP
.B int clock_getres(clockid_t clk_id, struct timespec *tp);
.PP
.B long getpid(void);
.PP
.B int getcpu(unsigned *cpu, unsigned *node);
.PP
.B uint64_t get_time_ns_tsc(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The SKYIOUS Kernel provides a Virtual Dynamic Shared Object (vDSO) as the
.B primary ABI
for time, process ID, and CPU ID queries. Unlike Linux where vDSO is an
optimization with syscalls as the primary interface, SKYIOUS userspace
libraries (vdso_libc, init, sash) use vDSO fast paths by default and fall
back to syscalls only when the vDSO page is unavailable.

Each CPU has its own 4 KiB vDSO page mapped at a fixed virtual address
range starting at \fB0x0000_7FFF_FFFC_0000\fR (256 KiB below USER_MMAP_TOP).
CPU N's page is at \fBVDSO_BASE + N * 4096\fR.

The vDSO data structure is updated by the kernel on timer ticks (100 Hz) and
context switches. Userspace reads use a seqlock protocol for consistency.
.SH DATA STRUCTURE
The vDSO page contains the following fields (offsets from page base):

.TS
l l l.
Offset Size Field Description
_
0x000 8 seq Seqlock for time fields (even=consistent, odd=updating)
0x008 8 monotonic_sec Monotonic clock seconds
0x010 4 monotonic_nsec Monotonic clock nanoseconds
0x018 8 realtime_sec Real-time clock seconds
0x020 4 realtime_nsec Real-time clock nanoseconds
0x028 8 tsc_freq_hz TSC frequency in Hz (read-only after init)
0x030 4 tsc_mult TSC multiplier (read-only after init)
0x034 4 tsc_shift TSC shift (read-only after init)
0x038 8 tsc_base TSC base at last sync
0x040 8 tsc_base_origin Original TSC base at boot (preserved across syncs)
0x048 8 pid_cpu_seq Seqlock for PID/CPU fields
0x050 8 pid Current process ID
0x058 4 cpu_id Current CPU ID
0x060 8 thread_cputime_ns Thread CPU time (CLOCK_SKYIOUS_THREAD_CPUTIME)
0x068 8 kernel_flags Kernel feature flags (CLOCK_SKYIOUS_KERNEL_FLAGS)
0x070 8 process_cgroup_id Cgroup ID (CLOCK_SKYIOUS_PROCESS_INFO)
0x078 8 process_namespace_flags Namespace flags (CLOCK_SKYIOUS_PROCESS_INFO)
0x080 8 process_cap_effective Effective capabilities (CLOCK_SKYIOUS_PROCESS_INFO)
0x088 4 tsc_sync_ack_count TSC sync IPI acknowledgment count
0x08C 4 tsc_sync_seq TSC sync sequence number
0x090 4 vdso_abi_version vDSO ABI version (monotonic integer)
.TE

.SH CLOCK IDS
The following clock IDs are supported via vDSO fast path:

.TS
l l l l.
ID Name Fast Path Notes
_
0 CLOCK_REALTIME Yes TSC + cached RTC interpolation
1 CLOCK_MONOTONIC Yes TSC-based (invariant)
5 CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE Yes 10ms resolution, cached realtime
6 CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE Yes 10ms resolution, tick-based
7 CLOCK_BOOTTIME Yes Same as MONOTONIC
4 CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW Yes Same as MONOTONIC
100 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_THREAD_CPUTIME No Syscall fallback (omits running slice)
101 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_SCHED_DEADLINE No Syscall fallback
102 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_BOOT_NS Yes TSC + tsc_base_origin (preserved across syncs)
103 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_KERNEL_FLAGS Yes Direct atomic load
104 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_PROCESS_INFO Yes Direct atomic loads
105 CLOCK_SKYIOUS_VDSO_VERSION Yes Direct atomic load (ABI version)
.TE

Clocks 2 (CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID) and 3 (CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID)
always use syscall fallback.

.SH SEQLK PROTOCOL
Two independent seqlocks protect different field groups:

.TP
.B seq (time fields)
Writer (timer tick, 100 Hz): increments to odd, writes monotonic_sec,
monotonic_nsec, realtime_sec, realtime_nsec, increments to even.
Reader: reads seq (Acquire), reads data (Relaxed), reads seq again
(Acquire), retries if seq changed or odd.

.TP
.B pid_cpu_seq (PID/CPU fields)
Writer (context switch): increments to odd, writes pid and cpu_id,
increments to even.
Reader: same protocol as time fields.

Userspace must also validate CPU identity: after reading cpu_id from the
vDSO page, compare it with the CPU obtained from the getcpu syscall (or
rdpid instruction). If they differ, the thread has migrated; retry with the
new CPU's page.

.SH FALLBACK PROTOCOL
If the vDSO page is not mapped (e.g., first access before kernel maps it,
or after mprotect/unmap), vdso_libc immediately falls back to the
corresponding syscall and logs a one-time message via the kernel serial
console. No retry loop is attempted — the page will not appear
spontaneously.

The fallback is also used for clocks not exposed via vDSO fast path.

.SH ABI VERSIONING
The vDSO ABI version is a monotonic integer stored in
\fBvdso_abi_version\fR (offset 0x090). Current version is 1.

Policy:
.RS
.IP \(bu
Increment ONLY on incompatible layout/semantic changes (field removal,
offset change, seqlock protocol change).
.IP \(bu
Compatible additions (new fields at end, new clock IDs, new kernel_flags
bits) do NOT bump version.
.IP \(bu
Userspace reads version at startup via CLOCK_SKYIOUS_VDSO_VERSION (105).
Mismatch → syscall-only fallback.
.RE

.SH AUXILIARY VECTOR
AT_SYSINFO_EHDR (type 33) points to VDSO_BASE (CPU 0's page). Userspace
computes per-CPU page address:
.EX
vdso_page(cpu_id) = AT_SYSINFO_EHDR + cpu_id * 4096
.EE

.SH TSC TIME CALCULATION
.nf
.elapsed = rdtsc() - tsc_base
.ns = (elapsed * tsc_mult) >> tsc_shift
.sec = ns / 1e9
.nsec = ns % 1e9
.fi

For CLOCK_REALTIME, the kernel caches a consistent (RTC sec, RTC nsec, TSC
timestamp) triple under a seqlock. Interpolation uses the cached TSC
timestamp, so TSC synchronization does not cause wall-clock steps.

.SH FILES
.B /lib/vdso_libc.a
Static archive providing vDSO primary ABI.

.B /etc/vdso.conf
Optional configuration: disable_vdso=0, log_fallback=1.

.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#include "vdso_libc.h"
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
    printf("MONOTONIC: %ld.%09ld\\n", ts.tv_sec, ts.tv_nsec);
    
    printf("PID: %ld\\n", getpid());
    
    unsigned cpu;
    getcpu(&cpu, NULL);
    printf("CPU: %u\\n", cpu);
    
    return 0;
}
.EE

